    "result_text": "Done. The full suite is green.\n\n## What I changed\n\nA single method in one file \u2014 `E164Telephone.toE164` in `src/main/java/.../rest/function/owner/E164Telephone.java`. The existing pipeline (`RequireOwnerFields` \u2192 `throw MissingOwnerFieldsException`) already turns a `null` from `toE164` into a **400**, so enforcing the rule inside `toE164` was enough \u2014 no new function classes, no YAML, no changes to the escalation handler.\n\nThe added check:\n\n```java\n// '+61' needs 9 national digits and '+1' 10 \u2014 each a total of 11 digits.\nboolean countryCoded = digits.startsWith(\"61\") || digits.startsWith(\"1\");\nreturn digits.matches(\"\\\\d{8,15}\") && (!countryCoded || digits.length() == 11)\n        ? \"+\" + digits : null;\n```\n\nFor `+61` and `+1` numbers, the total digit count must be exactly 11 (9 and 10 national digits respectively); other country codes keep the general 8\u201315 rule. Wrong-length country-coded numbers now return `null` \u2192 400.\n\n## Why this keeps structural impact low\n\n- **One file, one function.** The cost model multiplies by files touched and by each changed function's `WMC_other`. `E164Telephone` has no other real methods (just a private constructor), so `WMC_other \u2248 1`.\n- I rejected the alternatives \u2014 a new private helper or a new pipeline step \u2014 because a helper would raise `WMC_other` for the method that calls it (and add a whole new function's lines), and a pipeline step would touch the YAML plus a new class file, multiplying the total.\n- The edit is ~3 lines with modest added complexity, keeping `CC * changed_lines` small.\n\nThe `+61 123` test case was actually already rejected (5 digits fails the `\\d{8,15}` floor); this change additionally closes the real gap \u2014 country-coded numbers that are in the 8\u201315 range but wrong for their country (e.g. a `+61` number with 8 national digits).",
